  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A stray shower is possible this morning, \notherwise expect drying conditions with increasing sunshine \nafter morning fog and stratus dissipate. \n\nLatest sfc obs and satellite imagery show widespread stratus\nover the Laurel Highlands and areas of valley fog especially\nnorth of Interstate 80 as the sun rises this morning. The fog\nwill dissipate by early to mid morning, and the stratus will mix\nout by noon. Thunderstorms along and just ahead of the cold \nfront over the past few hours have since dissipated, but a stray\nshower can't be ruled out through the morning with lingering \ninstability and sfc moisture. Dry air advection and subsidence \ncapping will put an end to any shower chances this afternoon.\nCompared to conditions at sunrise, it should feel noticeably\nless humid by sunset this evening. We expect low humidity air to\nstick with us through Tuesday as high pressure builds in from\nthe north.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Warmth surges again later this week with \ndangerous heat possible Thursday and Friday.\n\nTemperatures will jump a few to several degrees into the upper \n80s and low 90s as we head into Tuesday, with a 592 dm ridge \nbuilding over the Mid Atl at 500 hPa. Humidity will likely stay\non the low side Tue before surging on Wed, when the sfc pattern\ntakes on a more Bermuda High regime and SW flow advects in \ndewpoints in the mid 60s. Thursday is progged to be the hottest \nday of the week, with highs likely climbing into the 90s for \nmuch of the area south/east of I-80/I-99. Combined with \ndewpoints in the upper 60s to near 70 degrees, this would \nresult in max Heat Index values approaching 100 degrees for the \nfirst time this year. Hot and humid conditions carry into Friday\nas well before we may get a bit of relief with a cold front for\nthe weekend, but even then temps are progged to remain above \nclimo.\n\nLatest NBM has 20-40 pct PoPs covering at least a portion of \ncentral PA each day Tuesday through the end of the coming week, \nstarting in west-central PA Tue and covering more of central PA\nWed-Fri as humidity increases. Uncertainty remains with how \nmuch precip there will be each day and if convection may be \nwidespread enough to erode the ridging, which would result in \nlower temperatures. As of right now it looks like convection\nwould be most widespread on Friday with the approach of the cold\nfront, but timing may change.",
